 Explain loop unrolling, common sub-expression elimination and dead code elimination.

 1. Loop unrolling
 The loop body is replicated so the loop runs fewer times: fewer condition tests, increments and jumps, and more straight-
 line code for other optimizations. Cost: larger code.


 int i = 1; int i = 1;
 while (i <= 100) { while (i <= 100) {
 a[i] = b[i]; a[i] = b[i];
 i++; i++;
 } a[i] = b[i];
 i++;
 100 tests + 100 jumps }


 50 tests + 50 jumps. If the count were not a multiple of 2 (or
 unknown), an extra clean-up iteration is needed.


 2. Common sub-expression elimination
 If an expression has been computed and none of its operands has changed since, its later occurrences reuse the earlier
 result instead of recomputing.


 t1 = b * c t1 = b * c
 a = t1 + d a = t1 + d
 t2 = b * c ; common e = t1 - f
 e = t2 - f
 Not allowed if b or c is assigned between the two b * c.


 3. Dead code elimination
 Code is dead if it can never execute (unreachable) or computes a value that is never used. It often appears after other
 optimizations (constant propagation, copy propagation).




